;;; NOISE

#|
Opus Mode provides various noise generators.
(See under Probability/Noise in the documentation.)

- White noise (gen-noise)
- Gaussian noise (gen-gaussian-noise)
- Pink noise (gen-pink-noise, pink-noise-sample)
|#

;;; ---------------------------------------------------------

;;; gen-white-noise / (list-plot)

#|
The function GEN-WHITE-NOISE generate N samples of white 
noise specified by the keyword :type.
Types are: :normal :binary :cauchy :chi-square-2 :exponential 
:extreme :gaussian :logistic :lognormal :triangular 
:low-pass :high-pass.
|#

;; normal
(list-plot (gen-white-noise 1000)
           :point-radius 2)

;; binary
(list-plot (gen-white-noise 100 :type :binary :seed 123)
           :join-points t :point-radius 1.5 :style :fill)

;; exponential (more lower then higher values)
(list-plot (gen-white-noise 100 :type :exponential)
           :join-points t :point-radius 1.5 :style :axis)

;; gaussian
(list-plot (gen-white-noise 100 :type :gaussian :seed 123)
           :join-points nil :point-radius 2)

;; There is another function only for gaussian noise
;; producing the similar result.
(list-plot (gen-gaussian-noise 100 :seed 123)
 :join-points nil :point-radius 2 )

;; high-pass
(list-plot (gen-white-noise 100 :type :high-pass :seed 123)
           :join-points t :point-radius 2)

;; low-pass
(list-plot (gen-white-noise 100 :type :low-pass)
           :join-points t :point-radius 2)

;;; ---------------------------------------------------------

;;; gen-pink-noise / pink-noise-sample

;;; ---------------------------------------------------------
;;; Meaning of numbers unclear
;;; ---------------------------------------------------------

#|
In pink noise, low frequencies are emphasized more than high 
ones. With each octave going up, the energy decreases by half. 
This sounds so balanced and pleasant to our ears because our 
auditory system works in a similar way — we do not perceive 
low and high tones equally. Pink noise appears surprisingly 
often in nature and everyday life, for example in the sound 
of wind, rain, or ocean waves.
The energy of pink noise is inversely proportional to 
frequency. Twice the frequency means half the energy. 
This is why pink noise is also called 1/f noise.
|#
